BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #565: Measuring Main Streets: Data Tools for Transit-
 Oriented Communities
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T210000
UID:e444d86b42b9a6dc3bd4640743c709dc@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:Topic: Measuring Main Streets: Data Tools for Transit-Oriented
  Communities\n\nUnderstanding what communities need from housing and trans
 it\, and deploying evidence-based responses to those needs is hard. Who's 
 living in housing around transit infrastructure? What amenities are in the
 ir neighbourhood\, and what's missing? What housing stock exists? How can 
 it be developed further to serve the local community effectively? And of c
 ourse\, how does walkability fit into this whole picture?\n\nTo equip comm
 unity leaders with tools to answer these questions and more\, the Canadian
  Urban Institute (CUI) developed Measuring Main Streets. It's a digital pl
 atform bringing together unique neighbourhood-level data on demographics\,
  housing development potential\, and community amenities in and around tra
 nsit station areas.\n\nWe'll demo the platform's tools and show how they g
 o beyond traditional transit-oriented development\, which tends to focus o
 n density\, to make the case for what we call transit-oriented complete co
 mmunities: places where transit investment also delivers the amenities and
  housing mix people actually need.\n\nSpeakers: Greg Spencer (he/him)\, Lu
 ca Carnegie (he/him)\n\nGreg has over 20 years of experience in urban and 
 economic development policy making and research. He has worked in consulti
 ng and academia roles across Canada\, the UK\, and Ireland helping local c
 ommunities reach their full potential. He has co-authored high profile rep
 orts with leading urban think tanks such as Brookings Metro\, The Martin P
 rosperity Institute\, and NESTA. He has also co-authored op-eds in the Glo
 be and Mail\, the Toronto Star\, and The Guardian. He holds a Ph.D. in Eco
 nomic Geography and a M.Sc. in Planning from the University of Toronto and
  a B.A. in Geography from Bishops University.\n\nLuca is a Data Analyst on
  the Data Analytics Team at the Canadian Urban Institute. After graduating
 \, he dove into urban data science at the CUI to drive evidenced-based\, m
 ultidisciplinary solutions to urban challenges. He holds a Bachelor of Inf
 ormation in Information Systems from the University of Toronto. In his fre
 e time\, he loves to watch and act in musical theatre\, play music\, and t
 est new recipes in his kitchen. This is his second time presenting at Civi
 c Tech and is SO excited to be back!!\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and 
 Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation and Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 
 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct: https://civictech.ca/about-us\n
 \n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tech Toronto Slack: https://link.ci
 victech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur weekly civic meetups bring toge
 ther Torontonians (designers\, coders\, urban planners\, government staff\
 , mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, communications strategists\, commun
 ity organizers\, and more) who share an interest in making Toronto more re
 sponsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and equitable through design\, tech\
 , and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\nFor more info: https://civictech
 .ca\n\n\\--\n\nSuggest a Speaker:&#x20\;\n\nInterested in speaking at a fu
 ture event\, or know someone who'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hear f
 rom you: https://link.civictech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nSuggest a Venue:
 &#x20\;\n\nHave a space that could host up to 80 civic-minded folks for an
  evening? Consider recommending your venue: https://link.civictech.ca/venu
 e-suggestions\n
